pub const REPLIES: &[&str] = &[
    "Stay in Le Marais for the centre and cafes, Saint-Germain for the classic postcard, or Canal Saint-Martin for cheaper nights and a younger crowd.",
    "Stay in the Eixample for a well-connected, walkable base (keep an eye on your belongings), Gracia for a village feel, or El Born for tapas and narrow streets.",
    "Stay in the centre by the Abbey to walk to the baths and the shops, or the Royal Crescent end for Georgian streets and quiet.",
    "Budapest is good with children who like water: Margaret Island has pools, bikes and a musical fountain. Check each bath's age rules before booking with children. The trams and metro are cheap and frequent.",
    "Cape Town is excellent with children if you have a car: the beaches, the cable car, the penguins at Boulders and the aquarium fill a week. Plan drives in daylight and stay on main roads where possible. For a township visit, go with a responsible, reliable tour guide.",
    "Rio is wonderful with children in the right places: the beaches, the cable car up Sugar Loaf and the botanical garden are the easy days. Take advice on which areas to walk and which to take a taxi through. Before swimming, get local advice and follow beach flags and lifeguard directions.",
    "Rome works with children if you keep the days short: the Colosseum and the Forum are one morning, the Pantheon and a gelato another, and Rome's public drinking fountains (nasoni) and water houses give you free drinking water. The cobbles are hard on a pram and the summer heat is real, so spring or autumn.",
    "Stay in the Jordaan for canals and quiet, De Pijp for the food market and locals, or the centre if you want everything on foot and do not mind noise.",
    "Stay in Monti for the Colosseum on foot and quiet nights, Trastevere for restaurants and cobbles, or Prati for the Vatican and a calmer evening.",
    "Stay in Shinjuku for the transport and the nightlife, Asakusa for the old town and quiet, or Shibuya if you want to be in the middle of it.",
    "Stay in Bloomsbury to walk to most of it, the South Bank for the river and theatres, or Shoreditch for bars and a late night.",
    "Book close to the middle of town and a street or two back from the main road: the station, the food and the evening are then walkable, and the rooms just off the front are quieter and cheaper. At the seaside that means a street back from the front rather than the front itself. Look at the map for a bar or a through road under the window, check the last bus or train if you will be out late, and book somewhere with free cancellation so you can move if the area is not what you hoped.",
    "Bath is a good day out rather than a week: the Roman Baths run a family trail with an audio guide for children, and the baths, the abbey and the river are all within a mile. It is fine with a pram in the centre and steep on the hills out of it.",
    "Barcelona is one of the easiest cities in Europe with children: the beach is on the metro, the Gothic Quarter is flat, and the Boqueria is a free hour of noise and fruit. The Sagrada Familia sells out, so book weeks ahead.",
    "London is one of the best cities in the world with children: free museums, the parks, and a ride on the top deck of a bus is a day out on its own. The tube is not step-free everywhere, so check the station before planning a pram route.",
    "Paris is good for families if you pick one thing a day: the Luxembourg gardens have boats and a playground, and the big museums are free for under-18s in the EU. Lunch is the easy meal out - restaurants are friendlier at midday than at eight.",
    "Lisbon is good with children if you take the trams and the funicular rather than walk the hills: the castle, the aquarium and Belem fill two easy days. The pavements are slippery when wet, and the hills are real.",
];

